# aclfile line for the MCP server's own Redis account in diagnose mode (Redis 7 or newer).
# Replace <SHA256-OF-PASSWORD>: aclfiles need hashed passwords,
#   echo -n 'the-password' | shasum -a 256
#
# Derived from what a bec_lib 4.1 BECClient executes, and checked against ACL LOG on a BEC 4.1
# stack with no denials:
# - %R~info/* %R~public/* %R~user/*   read access to BEC's endpoint prefixes; the prefix is the
#                                      ACL boundary (bec_lib/endpoints.py)
# - %W~user/services/*                 the client's own status and metrics (SET with EXPIRE)
# - %W~user/log                        bec_logger appends log records to this stream (XADD)
# - +@read +keys                       cached reads; bec_lib lists services with KEYS
# - +set +expire +xadd +multi +exec    the only writes the client makes, confined to the %W~ keys
# - +acl|whoami                        BECClient reads its own account name at start
# - &* +@pubsub -publish -spublish     subscribe to any channel, publish to none...
# - (+publish &user/services/*)        ...except the client's own status channels.
#
# PUBLISH must stay limited: BEC 4 takes scan requests as PUBLISH on
# personal/<user>/queue/queue_request and queue aborts and halts on
# user/queue/queue_modification_request. With &* and an unrestricted +publish this account could
# submit scans as any user or stop the queue. With this line both get NOPERM.
#
# After deploying, start the server with this account and check `redis-cli ACL LOG` for
# denials; extend the grant only for what a new bec_lib release needs.
user mcp_ro on #<SHA256-OF-PASSWORD> %R~info/* %R~public/* %R~user/* %W~user/services/* %W~user/log &* +@read +@connection +@pubsub -publish -spublish +keys +set +expire +xadd +multi +exec +acl|whoami (+publish &user/services/*)
